   "id": "syn-mu-2",
   "text": "Contract prices for memory chips fell for the first time in a year, according to industry trackers, as smartphone and PC makers reduced orders after building up inventory in the first half.\n\nThe drop weighed on shares of Micron Technology and its Asian competitors. Analysts said the decline was concentrated in commodity memory for consumer devices, while demand for the high-bandwidth memory used in data centres remained strong and supply tight.\n\nMicron had said at its last earnings call that it expected pricing to hold through the year. One analyst cut his earnings forecasts for the company, citing the risk that weaker consumer demand would spread to other product lines, but kept a positive rating on the stock, pointing to the data centre business.\n\nThe company declined to comment ahead of its results next month.",
   "context": "article",
   "source": {
    "title": "Memory prices slip as smartphone makers trim orders",
    "license": "written by thinkingtext, illustrative (not a news report)"
